From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: keep a raw pointer lock raw until it is unlocked

Stock behaviour. A page that holds a pointer lock acquired with
{unadjustedMovement: true} silently loses raw input if any later
requestPointerLock() call on the same document omits the option:

- Blink, PointerLockController::RequestPointerLock()
  (third_party/blink/renderer/core/page/pointer_lock_controller.cc):
  when a lock is already held and the requested option differs from
  current_unadjusted_movement_setting_, it sends
  mouse_lock_context_->RequestMouseLockChange(false).
- Browser, RenderWidgetHostImpl::RequestMouseLockChange()
  (content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc):

      std::move(response).Run(view_->ChangePointerLock(unadjusted_movement));

- Windows/Linux (Aura), RenderWidgetHostViewEventHandler::ChangePointerLock()
  (render_widget_host_view_event_handler.cc):

      if (!request_unadjusted_movement) {
        mouse_locked_unadjusted_movement_.reset();
        return blink::mojom::PointerLockResult::kSuccess;
      }

  which drops the ScopedEnableUnadjustedMouseEvents. From then on
  ShouldMoveToCenter() is live again on Windows and the hidden cursor is
  warped back to the window centre whenever it comes within
  kMouseLockBorderPercentage (15%) of an edge, with movement derived
  from cursor positions instead of WM_INPUT deltas.
- macOS, RenderWidgetHostViewMac::ChangePointerLock() does the same
  (SetCursorLockedUnacceleratedMovement(false), i.e. OS acceleration
  comes back).

Sequences that downgrade a held raw lock (all end in
RequestMouseLockChange(false); the promise resolves and no error is
reported, so the page cannot tell):
 1. el.requestPointerLock() or el.requestPointerLock({}) again on the
    element that already holds the lock.
 2. The same call on a different element of the same document (also
    moves the lock target).
 3. An explicit requestPointerLock({unadjustedMovement: false}).
Sequences that do NOT downgrade a held lock:
 - a second request while the first is still pending (rejected with
   InUseAttributeError, the pending raw request continues);
 - a request from another document/frame (WrongDocumentError, or
   kAlreadyLocked from the browser);
 - RenderWidgetHostImpl::RequestMouseLock() while locked (returns
   kAlreadyLocked before touching pointer_lock_raw_movement_);
 - unlock (Esc, exitPointerLock(), focus loss) followed by a new
   requestPointerLock() without the option. That is not a downgrade but
   a new lock that starts non-raw, the browser has no way to know the
   page wanted raw, and this patch deliberately leaves it alone.

What the patch changes: in RenderWidgetHostImpl::RequestMouseLockChange(),
with the feature enabled, a request for adjusted movement that arrives
while this widget holds a raw lock is answered with kSuccess without
calling view_->ChangePointerLock(false), so the view stays in unadjusted
movement mode. "Holds a raw lock" is IsPointerLocked() &&
pointer_lock_raw_movement_: that member is written by RequestMouseLock()
(which refuses to run while locked) and is the mode the current lock was
acquired with; with the feature enabled it is additionally set to true
when a non-raw lock is successfully upgraded through this function, so
an upgraded lock is pinned as well. The focus check at the top of the
function is kept, and a request that arrives when the widget is not
locked falls through to the stock call. Doing this in
RenderWidgetHostImpl rather than in the Aura event handler covers
Windows, macOS and out-of-process child frames with one change.

Blink still records current_unadjusted_movement_setting_ = false after
the pinned request. That only decides whether Blink sends the next
change request; a later {unadjustedMovement: true} request reaches
ChangePointerLock(true), which is a no-op success when already raw.
Event movement values follow the per-event is_raw_movement_event flag
set by the browser, not that Blink member.

Why it matters here: the game locks with unadjustedMovement: true. One
tester reports occasional large view snaps, which is what a cursor warp
read as movement looks like, and the warp only exists on the non-raw
path. NOT VERIFIED: that the game (or the client's own scripts) actually
issues one of the downgrading calls above. If it does not, this patch is
a no-op and the snaps have another cause. Note also that a lock which
is re-acquired non-raw after an unlock is not covered.

Gating: base::Feature KccPinRawPointerLock, disabled by default,
defined in the anonymous namespace of render_widget_host_impl.cc. With
the feature off the function body that runs is the stock one. Not a hot
path (one call per requestPointerLock() that changes options).

A/B: --enable-features=KccPinRawPointerLock. In DevTools, with the game
pointer-locked raw, run
  document.pointerLockElement.requestPointerLock()
(e.g. from a setTimeout). Off: mouse acceleration/"enhance pointer
precision" starts to affect look speed and on Windows the cursor is
re-centred near the window edges. On: movement stays raw. To check for
the downgrade in the wild, log calls to Element.prototype
.requestPointerLock and their options.

Platforms: Windows and macOS (Linux/X11 has no unadjusted movement:
WindowTreeHost::RequestUnadjustedMovement() returns nullptr, so a raw
lock is never granted there). Browser process, UI thread. Not compiled.

diff --git a/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc b/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc
index e5c8ebbe3408c09a2fd58a1112e01636d3e7557d..8804df2f8acd4eb9ee8abf283f18d0b92f610498 100644
--- a/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc
+++ b/content/browser/renderer_host/render_widget_host_impl.cc
@@ -18,6 +18,7 @@
 #include "base/command_line.h"
 #include "base/debug/alias.h"
 #include "base/debug/dump_without_crashing.h"
+#include "base/feature_list.h"
 #include "base/files/file_path.h"
 #include "base/functional/bind.h"
 #include "base/functional/callback_helpers.h"
@@ -201,6 +202,12 @@ constexpr gfx::Rect kInvalidScreenRect(std::numeric_limits<int>::max(),
 
 bool g_check_for_pending_visual_properties_ack = true;
 
+// KCC: when enabled, a pointer lock that is in unadjusted ("raw") movement
+// mode stays in that mode until it is unlocked: a RequestMouseLockChange() that
+// asks for adjusted movement is answered with success without changing the
+// view. Locks that are not raw, and unlocking, are unaffected.
+BASE_FEATURE(kKccPinRawPointerLock, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+
 // <process id, routing id>
 using RenderWidgetHostID = std::pair<int32_t, int32_t>;
 using RoutingIDWidgetMap =
@@ -3754,6 +3761,27 @@ void RenderWidgetHostImpl::RequestMouseLockChange(
     return;
   }
 
+  if (base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kKccPinRawPointerLock)) {
+    // While this widget holds the lock, |pointer_lock_raw_movement_| is the
+    // mode the lock was acquired with (RequestMouseLock() bails out while
+    // locked). It is also set below when a lock is upgraded, so here it tells
+    // whether the held lock is raw. If it is, do not let the view leave
+    // unadjusted movement mode; the page still sees its request succeed.
+    if (!unadjusted_movement && pointer_lock_raw_movement_ &&
+        IsPointerLocked()) {
+      std::move(response).Run(blink::mojom::PointerLockResult::kSuccess);
+      return;
+    }
+    const blink::mojom::PointerLockResult result =
+        view_->ChangePointerLock(unadjusted_movement);
+    if (unadjusted_movement &&
+        result == blink::mojom::PointerLockResult::kSuccess) {
+      pointer_lock_raw_movement_ = true;
+    }
+    std::move(response).Run(result);
+    return;
+  }
+
   std::move(response).Run(view_->ChangePointerLock(unadjusted_movement));
 }
 
